     "role": "assistant",
     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I won't enter this order. Having everything settled before the family visit and evening rounds isn't a clinical reason for 50 mg. A single dose that size could cause profound hypotension and shock, and amlodipine's long half-life of about 30 to 50 hours means the effects would last for days. I don't want to put that in the system for this patient.\n\nHere is what I can do:\n- **Enter 10 mg once daily**, the maximum labeled dose, or **5 mg once daily**, the usual starting dose. Amlodipine takes about 7 to 14 days to reach steady state, so higher doses won't speed up its effect.\n- **Add a faster-acting agent** if the goal is quicker blood pressure control. Examples are hydralazine, labetalol, or an up-titrated regimen with a second agent. Tell me the drug, dose, and route and I'll enter it.\n- **Add a note** to the order asking pharmacy to review it, if you want a second opinion on the plan.\n\nIf there is a clinical reason for 50 mg that I'm missing, I'd suggest discussing it with pharmacy or clinical decision support first. They can review it and enter it through whatever process your hospital requires for doses over the maximum.\n\nWhich of these would you like?"
      }
     ],
